WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.310
Error handling should be part of the application architecture, not a
scattering of one-off try and except blocks.

2
00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:21.493
A Python service normally has several layers that can shape errors:
request validation, domain logic, framework middleware, route handlers,
database drivers, background workers, queues, logging libraries, tracing
tools, alerting pipelines, and user interface code.

3
00:00:21.493 --> 00:00:27.211
Each layer can translate detail safely or accidentally pass sensitive
information to the wrong place.

4
00:00:27.211 --> 00:00:29.708
Trust boundaries define who should see what.

5
00:00:29.708 --> 00:00:32.075
User input crosses into application code.

6
00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:34.243
Application code calls dependencies.

7
00:00:34.243 --> 00:00:36.478
Framework handlers prepare responses.

8
00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:38.976
Logs and traces flow to centralized systems.

9
00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:45.548
Developers, administrators, support staff, and external clients have
different roles and different need-to-know.

10
00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:55.276
A safe architecture routes diagnostic detail toward controlled internal
channels while sending users a stable, limited explanation and a reference
identifier when useful.

11
00:00:55.276 --> 00:01:00.271
Expected and unexpected errors should be handled differently, but both
need discipline.

12
00:01:00.271 --> 00:01:06.055
Validation errors can often tell a user what field needs correction
without exposing internal schemas.

13
00:01:06.055 --> 00:01:11.905
Authentication and authorization failures should avoid revealing extra
account or permission detail.

14
00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:19.003
Dependency, database, file, queue, and third-party failures should usually
be translated into application-level messages.

15
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:24.393
Background job failures need durable records because there may be no user
waiting for a response.

16
00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:27.745
Centralizing error behavior makes secure defaults easier.

17
00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:39.050
Teams can define standard response shapes, exception classes, log fields,
redaction rules, retry boundaries, and alerting signals in one pattern
instead of relying on every developer to remember them.

18
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.468
That does not eliminate local judgment, but it reduces drift.

19
00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:48.120
When failure behavior is designed intentionally, it is easier to review,
test, monitor, and improve.
